<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bhagavad Gita</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gita</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bhagavad Gita is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> scripture of 700 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> verses forming part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> epic, probably composed in the last centuries BCE. It records a dialogue on the eve of battle between the prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arjuna">Arjuna</a> and the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> on duty, devotion and liberation. It is among the most widely read and commented religious texts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita.jpg" alt="Bhagavad Gita" width="320" height="194" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BhagavadGita-19th-century-Illustrated-Sanskrit-Chapter_1.20.21.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
